Source package: tinysparql

resource-print-null-on-invalid-input.patch
    Makes tracker_resource_print_turtle, tracker_resource_print_sparql_update and
    tracker_resource_print_jsonld return NULL when given something which is not a
    TrackerResource. They returned a static empty string, which a caller frees like any result
    of these functions, and freeing it aborted the program. The localsearch extractor crashed
    this way, see patches/localsearch/. The patch is the first commit of
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/tinysparql/-/merge_requests/819, including its test, which is
    added to tests/libtinysparql/tracker-resource-test.c and runs during the build.

service-test-wait-for-endpoint-loop.patch
    Fixes a race in tests/core/tracker-service-test.c, which runs during the build. Each of its
    tests runs a D-Bus endpoint in a thread with its own main loop, and quit and freed that loop
    without waiting for the thread. When the thread reached g_main_loop_run() only after that, GLib
    aborted the test, and with it the build, with "g_main_loop_run: assertion 'loop != NULL'
    failed". The test now waits until the loop runs, and joins the thread before it frees the
    loop. A 200 ms sleep before g_main_loop_run() makes the test fail every time without the patch
    and pass with it. The patch changes only the test and is not proposed upstream yet.

cursor-keeps-db-interface.patch
    Keeps the database interface alive as long as a cursor on it is open. A cursor's statement only
    borrows the interface, so closing a connection could free it while a worker thread still
    finalized a cursor, which then referenced freed memory. The D-Bus endpoint of the tinysparql
    command crashed this way when it received SIGTERM right after serving a query, which failed a
    build of the package in the functional test test_cli, and 10 of 2000 local runs of that test
    under load. The patch is the first commit of
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/tinysparql/-/merge_requests/820 by another contributor,
    including its test, which is added to tests/libtinysparql/tracker-sparql-test.c and runs during
    the build.

serialize-test-startup-errors.patch
    Makes tests/libtinysparql/tracker-serialize-test.c, which runs during the build, fail at once
    with the error when its D-Bus endpoint or its HTTP endpoint on the fixed port 54322 does not
    start. It waited for them forever, until meson killed it after 210 seconds, which failed a
    build of the package without saying why. The patch is upstream's commit 064f4379 from
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/tinysparql/-/merge_requests/831, with a declaration of a variable
    which a later upstream commit adds, and changes only the test.

test-ports-below-ephemeral-range.patch
    Makes the tests' HTTP endpoints listen on ports from 10000 to 31999, and on 32321 to 32323
    instead of the fixed 54321 to 54323, below the range from which Linux chooses the local ports
    of outgoing connections, 32768 to 60999. The tests' own HTTP connections keep their local ports
    in TIME_WAIT for a minute after they close, up to 71 at once during one run of the suite, and
    an endpoint cannot listen on such a port, so a test sometimes failed with "Address already in
    use", or waited until meson killed it for an endpoint which never started. The patch changes
    only the tests and is not proposed upstream yet.

Drop resource-print-null-on-invalid-input.patch once Ubuntu ships a version which includes merge
request 819. If upstream changed the commit before merging it, check first that all three
functions still return NULL there. Drop service-test-wait-for-endpoint-loop.patch once Ubuntu
ships a version whose tracker-service-test waits for the loop of the endpoint to run. Drop
cursor-keeps-db-interface.patch once Ubuntu ships a version which includes merge request 820, and
serialize-test-startup-errors.patch once it ships one which includes merge request 831. Drop
test-ports-below-ephemeral-range.patch once Ubuntu ships a version whose tests choose their HTTP
ports outside of that range or let the system choose them.
